title: The Bothy
start: start

== start ==
bg workshop_bench
music room_tone
The bothy is two rooms and a porch at the end of a track that stops being a
track about a mile before you get to it. There is a stove, a table, four bunks,
and a book with a pen on a string.
The book is for anybody who stays. You write the date, how many of you, and
where you came from. It is not a register and nobody reads it, except that
everybody reads it, because the first thing you do when you arrive is look at
who was here before.
You have been the volunteer warden for six years. That means you walk up on the
first Saturday of the month and check the stove and the roof and the book.
-> the_track

== the_track ==
Today is the first Saturday of the month and the track is wet and there is
nobody up here and there will not be anybody up here until April.
You have the key and the coal and a sandwich, and the walk in takes an hour and
forty minutes, and you have done it seventy-two times.
At the top of the last rise you can see the bothy, which is a low grey shape
with a green door, and you can see that the door is open.
-> the_door

== the_door ==
The door being open is not alarming. The latch is old and the wind takes it,
and you have found it open twice before, and both times you wrote it in your
own notebook and mentioned it to nobody.
You go in.
The stove is cold. The table is clear. The book is open at a page with writing
on it, which means somebody has been here since the last time you were.
-> the_book

== the_book ==
The last entry is dated nine days ago and it says: two of us, from the north.
The handwriting is small and careful and the pen is back on its string, which
is more than most people manage.
You have read a hundred of these. You have never met any of the people who
write them and you never will, and that is the whole arrangement: they get a
roof and a stove and a book, and you get to know that the roof held.
-> the_check

== the_check ==
You check the stove, which is drawing. You check the roof, which is not leaking
in the two places it leaked last year. You sweep the floor and you take the
ash out and you put the ash on the midden with the rest.
Then you sit at the table with your sandwich and the book, because this is the
part of the job you would not give up.
* Read back through the winter. -> read_back
* Write today's entry. -> write_today
* Neither. Just sit. -> just_sit

== read_back ==
You read back to November, which is as far as anybody has been this year.
Two of us, from the north. One of us, from the coast. Four of us, and one of
them is nine years old, and whoever wrote that has underlined the nine.
Two of us, from the north, again, in the same hand as today's.
You do the arithmetic without meaning to. Nine days ago was a Tuesday, and the
forecast that week was for the worst weather of the winter, and somebody walked
in here in it and wrote in the book and left the pen on the string.
-> the_write

== write_today ==
You write the date, and one of us, and where you came from, which is the town
at the bottom of the glen, and then you sit with the pen for a while because
there is a fifth column in this book that nobody has ever used and that you did
not put there.
It is headed, in a hand much older than yours, WENT OUT.
-> the_fifth

== just_sit ==
You sit. The stove ticks as it cools and the wind does what it does and there is
no sound in the bothy that you have not heard seventy-two times.
You are fifty-one years old and you have walked in here on the first Saturday
of every month for six years, and in that time you have spoken to four people
on this track, and none of them were here.
-> the_write

== the_fifth ==
You have looked at that column for six years and assumed it was for the warden
to fill in, and you have never filled it in, and neither has anybody else.
WENT OUT.
You write it. Today's date, one of us, and in the fifth column, in your own
hand, you write the town at the bottom of the glen.
~ wrote_went_out true
-> the_write

== the_write ==
You finish the sandwich and you wash the cup you did not use and you bank the
stove down to nothing, because that is what you do, and you leave the coal you
brought in a heap by the door for whoever is next.
You put the pen back on its string.
You close the book.
-> the_lock

== the_lock ==
You pull the door to and the latch takes it and you check it with your hand,
which you always do.
Halfway down the track you stop and look back, which you never do, and the
bothy is a low grey shape with a green door, and the door is shut.
In April somebody will walk in and read the book and see today's date and one of
us and the town at the bottom of the glen, and in the fifth column, WENT OUT.
They will not know what it means and they will not need to.
* Leave it at that. -> END
* Walk back up and write a note under it. -> the_note [if wrote_went_out]

== the_note ==
You walk back up, which takes an hour and forty minutes in the other direction
and is the only time you have ever done the track twice in one day.
You write, under WENT OUT, in the smallest hand you can manage: it means the
warden came up and went home again, and it has been empty for six years because
nobody ever wrote in it, and you are writing in it now so that the next person
who opens this book does not have to wonder.
Then you put the pen back on the string and you close the book and you walk
down again, and it is dark by the bottom, and the bothy has a light on it that
is the moon on the green door.
-> END
